{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.BringEllipticCurveJInvariant","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.BringEllipticCurveJInvariant","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Rat.Cast.Order"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.not_cm_j_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.not_cm_j_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BringEllipticCurveJInvariant.lean#L92-L101","doc":"**`−25/2` is not an integer**, hence not a CM `j`-invariant: every\ncomplex-multiplication `j` is a rational integer. So Serre's `E` has no CM, its\nmodular parameter has no closed form, and it supplies no *derived* phase. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.bring_j_five_tau_zero_not_integral","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.bring_j_five_tau_zero_not_integral","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BringEllipticCurveJInvariant.lean#L77-L90","doc":"**Neither invariant is an integer, so `τ₀` is not a CM point.** Both have\nstrictly negative 2-adic valuation — `v₂(j(τ₀)) = −1`, `v₂(j(5τ₀)) = −5` — which\nis potentially *multiplicative* reduction at 2 (Tate curves), the exact opposite\nof what CM forces (integral `j`, hence potentially good reduction everywhere).\n\nThis settles the question the arc reached branch 3 with: `τ₀` was the one\nremaining hope for a canonical phase, on the grounds that a period-matrix\nparameter might be a CM point where Serre's isogeny factor is not. It is not. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.bring_j_five_tau_zero_decimal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.bring_j_five_tau_zero_decimal","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BringEllipticCurveJInvariant.lean#L73-L75","doc":"Its decimal form, which is what a numerical root-finder returns. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.bring_j_five_tau_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.bring_j_five_tau_zero","kind":"theorem","line":63,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BringEllipticCurveJInvariant.lean#L63-L71","doc":"**Theorem 2's second invariant, decoded and derived.** Riera–Rodríguez pin\n`τ₀` modulo `Γ₀(5)` by the pair `j(τ₀)`, `j(5τ₀)`. The library scan garbles the\nsecond as `- 293x5` with a stray `25` on the following line; computing the six\nroots of `Φ₅(−25/2, Y)` independently gives `−3810.78125` among them, which is\nexactly `−5·29³/2⁵` — the fragment being `−29³·5` over `2⁵`, the exponent having\nbeen OCR'd onto its own line. So the derivation supplies the value and the scan\nonly selects it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.bring_serre_nonsingular","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.bring_serre_nonsingular","kind":"theorem","line":57,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BringEllipticCurveJInvariant.lean#L57-L61","doc":"The discriminant denominator is nonzero, so the quotient above is the\n`j`-invariant of a genuine elliptic curve rather than a degenerate cubic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.bring_serre_j_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.bring_serre_j_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BringEllipticCurveJInvariant.lean#L51-L55","doc":"The `j`-invariant of `y² = x³ + Ax + B` is `1728·4A³/(4A³ + 27B²)`. For\nSerre's curve `A = −675`, `B = −79650` this is exactly `−25/2`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}